Actually, for an HTML/CSS/JavaScript repository, I would simplify the approach significantly. You don't need architecture documents about APIs or databases—the biggest challenge is helping the agent locate the correct files and understand the relationships between them.

If I were building a coding agent that only has GitHub get_content, I'd organize .ai like this:

.ai/
├── index.md                ← Entry point
├── project_map.md          ← Folder and feature map
├── file_catalog.md         ← Important files and their purpose
├── ui_components.md        ← Reusable UI patterns
├── coding_rules.md         ← Project conventions
├── workflows.md            ← Common implementation patterns
├── dependencies.md         ← Which files depend on which
├── common_tasks.md         ← How to perform frequent changes
└── pitfalls.md             ← Known gotchas

1. index.md

This is the first file the agent always reads.

Example:

Project Overview

Tech Stack
- HTML
- CSS
- JavaScript

Important Documents

Project Structure -> project_map.md

Important Files -> file_catalog.md

Coding Rules -> coding_rules.md

UI Components -> ui_components.md

Task Routing

UI change
→ ui_components.md

Styling
→ coding_rules.md

Feature addition
→ common_tasks.md

Bug fix
→ dependencies.md


