What I'd generate automatically

Since you can call GitHub get_content, I'd have a scheduled process generate a lightweight manifest rather than asking the agent to inspect every file.

Example:

{
  "dashboard.js": {
    "purpose": "Dashboard interactions",
    "depends_on": [
      "chart.js",
      "utils.js"
    ],
    "exports": [
      "renderDashboard"
    ]
  },
  "navbar.js": {
    "purpose": "Navigation",
    "depends_on": [
      "utils.js"
    ]
  }
}

The agent reads this manifest first, then fetches only the handful of source files it actually needs.

The biggest improvement I'd make

Because you mentioned many large JavaScript files, I would avoid treating each file as a single unit. Instead, maintain an index of logical sections within large files. For example:

dashboard.js

Lines 1–120
Initialization

Lines 121–260
Filters

Lines 261–480
Chart rendering

Lines 481–620
Export functionality

Lines 621–900
Event handlers

Or even better, index by function names and responsibilities if they're well defined. That way, when the user asks "add a filter," your agent knows to fetch and focus on the "Filters" section instead of reasoning over a 900-line file. This dramatically improves accuracy while keeping get_content usage efficient.
